<chapter
 id="lecture07">
#title("Functional List-Processing (3)")

#para('\

In this chapter, I intend to present a few more list-processing
functions. More importantly, I would like to argue for the practice of
actively identifying the need for generic list-processing functions
during problem-solving. As in the previous chapters, the lists are
linear lists: a function borrows the lists it reads and constructs the lists
it returns, which are to be freed by the caller; a list of lists is
linear, too, and it has to be freed as a whole.

')

#para('\

There are many useful list-processing functions for handling two lists
simultaneously. For instance, the following function #dyncode("list_zip")
takes a pair of lists and returns a list of pairs (the pairs are flat tuples):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a,b:t@ype}
list_zip
  {m,n:nat}
( xs: !list_vt(a, m)
, ys: !list_vt(b, n)): List0_vt(@(a, b))
//
implement
{a,b}(*tmp*)
list_zip(xs, ys) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() =>
  list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  (
    case+ ys of
    | list_vt_nil() =>
      list_vt_nil()
    | list_vt_cons(y, ys) =>
      list_vt_cons(@(x, y), list_zip<a,b>(xs, ys))
  )
)
//
')

For instance, given two lists
#dyncode("(1, 3, 5)") and #dyncode("(2, 4)"), #dyncode("list_zip")
returns the list consisting of #dyncode("@(1, 2)") and
#dyncode("@(3,4)").  Note that #dyncode("@(...)") is the notation for
constructing flat tuples. The dependent types #stacode("list_vt(a, m)") and
#stacode("list_vt(b, n)") in the interface tell that the arguments are
lists of lengths m and n; the length of the result is not specified as it is
the smaller of m and n. For a list-processing function handling one
list, there is often a meaningful variant that essentially acts like
passing to the list-processing function a list of pairs obtained from
applying #dyncode("list_zip") to two given lists. As an example,
the following function #dyncode("list_map2") is such a variant
of #dyncode("list_map"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun {
a,b:t@ype}{c:t@ype
} list_map2
( xs: !List0_vt(a)
, ys: !List0_vt(b)
, fopr: &(a, b) -<clo1> c): List0_vt(c)
//
implement
{a,b}{c}
list_map2
(xs, ys, fopr) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() =>
  list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  (
    case+ ys of
    | list_vt_nil() =>
      list_vt_nil()
    | list_vt_cons(y, ys) =>
      list_vt_cons(fopr(x, y), list_map2<a,b><c>(xs, ys, fopr))
  ) (* end of [list_vt_cons] *)
)
//
')

For instance, given two lists
#dyncode("(1, 3, 5)") and #dyncode("(2, 4, 6)")
and the multiplication function, #dyncode("list_map2") returns the
list #dyncode("(2, 12, 30)"). Sometimes, #dyncode("list_map2") is
referred to as #dyncode("list_zipwith").

')

#para('\

There are various list-processing functions operating on ordered
lists. For instance, the following function #dyncode("list_merge") is
for merging two ordered lists into one. The type of the function tells that the
length of the result is the sum of the lengths of the two arguments, which are
only borrowed (the elements are copied to the result):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_merge
  {m,n:nat}
( xs: !list_vt(a, m)
, ys: !list_vt(a, n)
, cmp: &(a, a) -<clo1> int): list_vt(a, m+n)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_merge
(xs0, ys0, cmp) = let
//
fun
auxlst
{m,n:nat}
( xs0: !list_vt(a, m)
, ys0: !list_vt(a, n)
, cmp: &(a, a) -<clo1> int
) : list_vt(a, m+n) = (
//
case+ xs0 of
| list_vt_nil() => list_vt_copy<a>(ys0)
| list_vt_cons
    (x1, xs1) => (
    case+ ys0 of
    | list_vt_nil() => list_vt_copy<a>(xs0)
    | list_vt_cons
        (y1, ys1) => let
        val sgn = cmp(x1, y1)
      in
        if (sgn <= 0)
          then list_vt_cons(x1, auxlst(xs1, ys0, cmp))
          else list_vt_cons(y1, auxlst(xs0, ys1, cmp))
        // end of [if]
      end // end of [list_vt_cons]
  ) (* end of [list_vt_cons] *)
//
) (* end of [auxlst] *)
//
in
  auxlst(xs0, ys0, cmp)
end // end of [list_merge]
//
')

Both #dyncode("list_take(xs, k)") and #dyncode("list_drop(xs, k)") are given
dependent types, which tell that #dyncode("k") is at most the length of
#dyncode("xs"). The former returns the prefix of
#dyncode("xs") that is of length #dyncode("k") and the latter the suffix of #dyncode("xs")
that excludes the first #dyncode("k") elements of #dyncode("xs"); both construct
a new list. (In the GC era, the functions were partial and raised exceptions
when the number of elements was out of range.)

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_take
  {n,k:nat | k <= n}
(xs: !list_vt(a, n), k: int(k)): list_vt(a, k)
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_drop
  {n,k:nat | k <= n}
(xs: !list_vt(a, n), k: int(k)): list_vt(a, n-k)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_take(xs, k) =
(
if k > 0
  then let
    val+list_vt_cons(x, xs) = xs
  in
    list_vt_cons(x, list_take<a>(xs, k-1))
  end
  else list_vt_nil()
)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_drop(xs, k) =
(
if k > 0
  then let
    val+list_vt_cons(_, xs) = xs
  in
    list_drop<a>(xs, k-1)
  end
  else list_vt_copy<a>(xs)
)
//
')

With #dyncode("list_merge"), we can readily implement
as follows the well-known mergesort algorithm to sort a given
list:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_mergesort
(xs: !List0_vt(a), cmp: &(a, a) -<clo1> int): List0_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_mergesort
  (xs, cmp) = let
//
// [msort]:
// the second argument is the length of the first one
//
fun
msort
{n:nat}
( xs: !list_vt(a, n)
, n: int(n)
, cmp: &(a, a) -<clo1> int): list_vt(a, n) =
if
(n >= 2)
then let
  val n1 = n / 2
  val xs1 = list_take<a>(xs, n1)
  val xs2 = list_drop<a>(xs, n1)
  val ys1 = msort(xs1, n1, cmp)
  val ys2 = msort(xs2, n-n1, cmp)
  val res = list_merge<a>(ys1, ys2, cmp)
  val () = list_vt_free<a>(xs1)
  val () = list_vt_free<a>(xs2)
  val () = list_vt_free<a>(ys1)
  val () = list_vt_free<a>(ys2)
in
  res
end // end of [then]
else list_vt_copy<a>(xs) // end of [else]
//
in
  msort(xs, list_length<a>(xs), cmp)
end // end of [list_mergesort]
//
')

It is guaranteed that #dyncode("list_mergesort") is log-linear (that is,
it is of O(n(log(n)))-time for n being the length of its
argument). Also, #dyncode("list_mergesort") is stable in the sense
that the order of the elements considered equal in the input is not
changed in the output. All of the intermediate lists are freed as soon as they
are no longer needed, and the sorted list is returned. Clearly,
#dyncode("list_merge") is not
tail-recursive, potentially running the risk of stack overflow when
#dyncode("list_mergesort") is applied to a long list (e.g., one
containing 1 million elements). This is a very serious issue with
non-tail-recursion in practice, and some approaches to addressing it are
to be presented later. In practice, a linear list can be sorted in place
without copying its elements, by the function #dyncode("list_vt_mergesort") of the
prelude, which consumes its argument, and the functions of this chapter are
meant to show how functional list-processing is done.

')

#para('\

During problem-solving, it often pays if one actively looks for
opptunities to generalize a specific function into one that can be
given a meaningful description in a broader context. As an example, if
one encounters a need to process all of the pairs formed with elements
chosen from a given list, then one may want to implement the following
function #dyncode("list_choose2"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_choose2
(xs: !List0_vt(a)): List0_vt(@(a, a))
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_choose2
  (xs) = let
//
typedef aa = @(a, a)
//
in
//
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() =>
  list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x0, xs) => let
    var fopr = lam@(x: a): aa => @(x0, x)
    val res1 = list_map<a><aa>(xs, fopr)
    val res2 = list_choose2<a>(xs)
  in
    list_vt_append<aa>(res1, res2)
  end // end of [list_vt_cons]
//
end // end of [list_choose2]
//
')

For instance, given the list #dyncode("(1, 2, 3)"),
#dyncode("list_choose2") returns the list of 3 pairs: #dyncode("(1,
2)"), #dyncode("(1, 3)"), and #dyncode("(2, 3)"). And
#dyncode("list_choose2") can be further generalized into the
following function #dyncode("list_nchoose") for listing all of the
tuples of a given length that are formed with elements chosen from a
given list. A list of lists is linear and the elements of the result are
lists that are all owned by the result; the function
#dyncode("lists_free") frees such a result, and the function #dyncode("list_mapcons")
consumes the list of lists that is passed to it and prepends its first argument to
each element in it:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// A list of lists is linear and it is freed (or consumed) as a whole
//
fun
{a:t@ype}
lists_free
(xss: List0_vt(List0_vt(a))): void =
(
case+ xss of
| ~list_vt_nil() => ()
| ~list_vt_cons(xs, xss) => (list_vt_free<a>(xs); lists_free<a>(xss))
)
//
// [list_mapcons] consumes its second argument:
// the first argument is added to the front of each list in it
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_mapcons
(x0: a, xss: List0_vt(List0_vt(a))): List0_vt(List0_vt(a))
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_mapcons(x0, xss) =
(
case+ xss of
| ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(xs, xss) =>
  list_vt_cons(list_vt_cons(x0, xs), list_mapcons<a>(x0, xss))
)
//
')

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_nchoose
(xs: !List0_vt(a), n: int): List0_vt(List0_vt(a))
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_nchoose
  (xs, n) =
  auxlst(xs, n) where
{
//
vtypedef xs = List0_vt(a)
//
fun
auxlst
(
xs: !xs, n: int
) : List0_vt(xs) =
(
if
(n <= 0)
then
list_vt_sing(list_vt_nil())
else
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x0, xs) =>
  list_vt_append<xs>(list_mapcons<a>(x0, auxlst(xs, n-1)), auxlst(xs, n))
) (* end of [else] *)
)
//
} (* end of [list_nchoose] *)
//
')

')

#para('\

Sometimes, we may need to process tuples consisting of elements
chosen from a given list as well as the complements of these tuples.
The following function #dyncode("list_nchoose_rest") generalizes
#dyncode("list_nchoose") in this regard. The elements of the result are
flat tuples that contain two linear lists, and the result is consumed
step by step by the two helper functions (a pattern in a flat tuple opens it):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// [list_nchoose_rest] is a generalization of [list_nchoose]:
// the pairs are flat tuples, which consist of two linear lists
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_nchoose_rest
(xs: !List0_vt(a), n: int): List0_vt(@(List0_vt(a), List0_vt(a)))
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_nchoose_rest
  (xs, n) =
  auxlst(xs, n) where
{
//
vtypedef xs = List0_vt(a)
vtypedef xsxs = @(xs, xs)
//
// the first component of each pair of a list is extended
//
fun
cons1
(x0: a, res: List0_vt(xsxs)): List0_vt(xsxs) =
(
case+ res of
| ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(@(xs1, xs2), res) =>
  list_vt_cons(@(list_vt_cons(x0, xs1), xs2), cons1(x0, res))
)
//
// the second component of each pair of a list is extended
//
fun
cons2
(x0: a, res: List0_vt(xsxs)): List0_vt(xsxs) =
(
case+ res of
| ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(@(xs1, xs2), res) =>
  list_vt_cons(@(xs1, list_vt_cons(x0, xs2)), cons2(x0, res))
)
//
fun
auxlst
(
xs: !xs, n: int
) : List0_vt(xsxs) =
(
if
(n <= 0)
then
list_vt_cons(@(list_vt_nil(), list_vt_copy<a>(xs)), list_vt_nil())
else
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x0, xs) => let
    val res1 = cons1(x0, auxlst(xs, n-1))
    val res2 = cons2(x0, auxlst(xs, n-0))
  in
    list_vt_append<xsxs>(res1, res2)
  end // end of [list_vt_cons]
) (* end of [else] *)
)
//
} (* end of [list_nchoose_rest] *)
//
')

For instance, given the list #dyncode("(1, 2, 3)") and integer
#dyncode("2"), #dyncode("list_nchoose_rest") returns the list of 3
pairs: #dyncode("@((1, 2), (3))"), #dyncode("@((1, 3), (2))"),
and #dyncode("@((2, 3), (1))").

')

#para('\

The problem of enumerating all of the permutations of a given
list is often used to test one\'s ability in constructing
recursively defined functions. Let us see a solution to this
problem given as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_permute
(xs: !List0_vt(a)): List0_vt(List0_vt(a))
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_permute
  (xs) = (
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() =>
  list_vt_sing(list_vt_nil())
| list_vt_cons _ => let
    vtypedef xs = List0_vt(a)
    vtypedef xsxs = @(xs, xs)
//
    fun
    auxlst
    (yszs: List0_vt(xsxs)): List0_vt(xs) =
    (
      case+ yszs of
      | ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
      | ~list_vt_cons(@(ys, zs), yszs) => let
          val-~list_vt_cons(y, ys) = ys
          val () = list_vt_free<a>(ys)
          val res = list_mapcons<a>(y, list_permute<a>(zs))
          val () = list_vt_free<a>(zs)
        in
          list_vt_append<xs>(res, auxlst(yszs))
        end // end of [list_vt_cons]
    ) (* end of [auxlst] *)
//
  in
    auxlst(list_nchoose_rest<a>(xs, 1))
  end (* end of [list_vt_cons] *)
)
//
')

For instance, given the list #dyncode("(1, 2, 3)"),
#dyncode("list_permute") returns a list of length 6 in which each
element is a permutation of #dyncode("(1, 2, 3)").  Note that
the first element of the pair in each element of
the list returned by #dyncode("list_nchoose_rest(xs, 1)") is a list of length 1:
its only element is
used for a permutation of the rest, and the temporary lists are freed.
A demo of the GC-era #dyncode("list_permute") can be seen by following this <ulink
url=\"https://ats-lang.github.io/DOCUMENT/ATS2FUNCRASH/LECTURE/07/CODE/Permute.html\">link</ulink>.

')

#para('\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/07/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter. The test in #filename("lecture07.dats") was
compiled with #command("patscc") (no flag is needed) and run with the address
sanitizer, which reports no leak. \

')#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture07"-->

